.TH CUSTODIO 1 "" "custodio" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
custodio \- import, list and delete keys of the custodio signing service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B custodio import
[\fB\-\-id\fR \fIkey id\fR]
.I file.p12
.br
.B custodio list
.br
.B custodio delete
.I key id
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B custodio
is the command-line tool of the custodio signing service.
.B import
reads a PKCS#12 file and stores its key where the service keeps keys.
The file password is asked on the terminal.
It is never taken from the arguments or from the environment.
.PP
.B list
shows each of your keys, in order of key id.
For every key it prints four lines: its
.I id
(the key id),
its
.I type
(\fBrsa2048\fR or \fBecdsa\-p256\fR),
the
.I subject
of its certificate, and the end of its validity
.RI ( "valid until" ,
the certificate's notAfter, in UTC).
If a certificate cannot be read, the key is still listed, its subject and end of validity
are shown as "certificate unreadable", and the exit status is 1.
Control characters in any of these texts are shown as \fB\\x\fINN\fR escapes.
Only your own keys are listed.
If you have none, it prints a sentence saying so and exits with status 0.
.B list
takes no arguments and does not ask for authorization: it never calls polkit.
.PP
.B delete
deletes your key
.IR "key id" ,
together with its certificate.
Before it calls the service it asks on the terminal and names the key.
The question says that the key cannot be recovered.
Only the answer \fBy\fR or \fByes\fR continues; anything else, an empty answer and the end of input included,
cancels: nothing is deleted and the exit status is 1.
There is no option, environment variable or configuration that skips the confirmation.
It is read from the controlling terminal, never from standard input,
so without a terminal
.B delete
refuses and deletes nothing.
After you confirm, polkit asks for authorization as well; if it is refused, nothing is deleted.
.B delete
takes exactly one argument, the key id, and no options;
to delete a key whose id starts with a dash, write
.B \-\-
before it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command succeeded; for
.BR list ,
also when there are no keys.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed: the service or the system bus could not be reached, the service answered with an error
(whose message is shown on standard error), a certificate could not be read, you did not confirm a
.BR delete ,
or there is no terminal to confirm it on.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is wrong.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-id " key id"
Name the stored key
.IR "key id" .
